What are Spinal Anesthesia Needles?

Spinal anesthesia needles are specialized medical devices engineered for the precise delivery of anesthetic agents into the cerebrospinal fluid within the subarachnoid space. Their unique beveled tip geometry is crucial for separating dural fibers rather than cutting them, which significantly minimizes the risk of post-dural puncture headaches. Marketed primarily by leaders like B. Braun and BD, these needles feature varied gauge sizes and tip designs that enhance procedural accuracy while reducing patient trauma.

These medical instruments have evolved substantially from conventional designs to include atraumatic pencil-point and Whitacre needles, which have demonstrated a reduction in headache incidence rates from over 36% with traditional needles to below 3% with modern versions. The transition toward finer gauges (24G-27G) represents a major clinical advancement, though it requires greater technical expertise from practitioners.

